   157	## 2. Lemma 3.12: local fork census
   158	
   159	### 2.1 The missing 35-to-20 filter
   160	
   161	The proof begins, “We omit the details since they are analogous to [4],
   162	Lemma 8” (p. 11).  That analogy is not itself a replay because part I had a
   163	stronger coprimality situation.  The needed standalone replacement is the
   164	following denominator-cancellation argument.
   165	
   166	Let a left edge-type block of degree (e) have image determinant (P), and
   167	let (n_b=1) if it has no second nonconstant endpoint.  Definition 3.2(b)
   168	gives
   169	
   170	\[
   171	 e\mid n(\widetilde a)n_bP.                              \tag{L1}
   172	\]
   173	
   174	All 15 signatures absent from Fig. 8 have (n(\widetilde a)=1).  If
   175	(e=3), cancellation of the factor 3 would require (3\mid n_b); a second
   176	endpoint is a fork, so its degree would be at least (2\cdot3=6), contrary
   177	to (3.1).  Thus a degree-3 left block forces (3\mid P).  An unsplit
   178	degree-4 block forces (2\mid P), because an endpoint fork of degree at most
   179	four has (n_b\le2), cancelling at most one factor 2.  Finally, a degree-2
   180	block in a split direction forces (2\mid P): otherwise (n_b=2), so that
   181	fork already has degree four, while the parallel positive-degree branch
   182	supplies another sheet at the same downstream target vertex by (3.2),
   183	contradicting (3.1).
   184	
   185	For a lower block (P=\det D_a).  Assertion 3.11(a) says that a left-image
   186	determinant (P_L) is coprime to (\det D_a).  Consequently the partition
   187	types impose these tests:
   188	
   189	| partition in a controlled (L) or (D) direction | forced condition |
   190	|---|---|
   191	| `4` | the relevant target determinant is even |
   192	| `31` | it is divisible by 3 |
   193	| `22` | it is even |
   194	| `211` | it is even; its two degree-one blocks also use the sole possible exceptional pair |
   195	| `1111` | impossible if all are controlled branches: four equal determinants (>1) give more than one bad pair |
   196	
   197	The two absent ((3,1)) rows are now forced out.  In `3|111|3`, both the
   198	left and lower unsplit degree-3 blocks force a factor 3.  In `21|3|21`, the
   199	degree-2 blocks in both controlled directions force a factor 2.  Each
   200	contradicts Assertion 3.11(a); the cancellation alternatives were excluded
   201	above.
   202	
   203	Here is the complete check of the 13 absent ((4,1)) rows.  The last column
   204	shows the common prime forced into the left-image determinant and
   205	(\det D_a).
   206	
   207	| absent (L|R|D) | prime | absent (L|R|D) | prime |
   208	|---|---:|---|---:|
   209	| `4|31|211` | 2 | `4|22|211` | 2 |
   210	| `4|211|22` | 2 | `4|1111|4` | 2 |
   211	| `31|31|31` | 3 | `31|22|31` | 3 |
   212	| `22|4|211` | 2 | `22|31|22` | 2 |
   213	| `22|22|22` | 2 | `22|211|4` | 2 |
   214	| `211|4|22` | 2 | `211|31|4` | 2 |
   215	| `211|22|4` | 2 |  |  |
   216	
   217	The (\widetilde g_1) exception does not save a `211` row: its repeated
   218	degree-one pair consumes that exception, while its parallel degree-2 block
   219	still forces evenness.  Thus `211` can face only `31` across the two
   220	controlled directions (the printed k and l); `4` can face `31` (j and m);
   520	| 1b | r–j | 4 |
   521	| 1c | t–j | 4 |
   522	| 1d | m | — |
   523	| 2a | l–j | 4 |
   524	| 2b | k–j | 4 |
   525	| 3a | o–g | 3 |
   526	| 3b | n–g | 3 |
   527	| 4a | g–f | 2 |
   528	| 4b | f–f | 2 |
   529	
   530	These are precisely the ten drawings on p. 15.  The drawings do not encode
   531	a complete placement of \(\widetilde g_1\): Lemma 3.12 fixes it for the
   532	suppressed a/c blocks and for l, k, r, while the Fig. 11 quotient leaves it
   533	undrawn in the other rows.  Lemma 3.14 is exhaustive as a census of the
