   542	## 4. Root/branch-location ledger for §§1–4
   543	
   544	The rooted-tree facts used here are [4], Lemma 3 (away from the root, all
   545	non-root branches but at most one have determinant one; at the root every
   546	branch does) and [4], Lemma 4 (a subgraph not containing the root has positive
   547	determinant).  These are generic determinant facts, not the defective
   548	one-dicritical inference discussed in §5.
   549	
   550	### 4.1 Section 1 and the auxiliary root calculations
   551	
   552	**R-1, target-root trichotomy (p. 3) — REPLAYED-SOUND.**  In Proposition
   553	1.3 the component (l_0) containing the unique infinity point of (g_1) is
   554	either nodal, the root, or non-root and non-nodal.  Resolving the (g_1)-germ
   555	at these three locations gives respectively Fig. 1a, Fig. 1b′, and Fig. 1b.
   556	The three cases are mutually exclusive and exhaustive.
   557	
   558	**R-2, (\widetilde v\in\widetilde L_\infty) (p. 3) —
   559	REPLAYED-SOUND.**  The root is the strict transform of the original source
   560	line at infinity.  At its generic point the homogenized target coordinate is
   561	zero; resolving indeterminacies does not change that generic image.  It
   562	therefore lies in (F^{-1}(L)=\widetilde L_\infty).
   563	
   564	**R-2a, terminal linear chains (Lemma 1.5, p. 3) —
   565	REPLAYED-SOUND.**  On a linear constant-degree chain ending at a dicritical,
   566	[4], Proposition 1 identifies the chain degree with the transverse order at
   567	that terminal flag. Proposition 1.2 gives
   568	\(n(\widetilde g_1)=1\) and \(n(\widetilde g_2)=2\); these, rather than the
   569	physical target places, are the two asserted chain degrees.
   570	
   571	**R-3, Lemmas 2.3–2.4 (p. 4, (2.1)–(2.2)) — REPLAYED-SOUND.**  Substitution
   572	in the displayed equations gives
   573	
   574	\[
   575	-1=p_1-dp_2,\quad
   576	0=-2-p_1+(d-1)(p_2-1)+d\Sigma
   577	\quad\Longrightarrow\quad p_2=d(\Sigma-1),
   578	\]
   579	
   580	contrary to the branch coprimality in [4], Lemma 2.  In Lemma 2.4 the
   581	canonical formula gives (p_1=-2), and the edge formula gives
   582	(p_2=1-2p_3<0).  The parenthetical “automatic if
   583	((\widetilde v\widetilde q)) is linear” is sound because the open path then
   584	has no nodal, hence no essential, vertex.
   585	
   586	**R-4, Lemmas 2.5–2.6 (p. 4) — REPAIRED.**  The paper merely calls them
   587	analogous.  For 2.5, under the contrary assumption,
   588	
   589	\[
   590	-2=p_1-dp_2,qquad
   591	1=-2-p_1+(d-1)(p_2-1)+d\Sigma
   592	\]
   593	
   594	again gives (p_2=d(\Sigma-1)), impossible.  For 2.6, absence of essential
   595	(\widetilde g_2)-contributions toward the root gives (p_1=-3); if
   596	(p_3=\det\operatorname {br}_{\widetilde q}(\widetilde g_2)>0), the edge
   597	formula is
   598	
   599	\[
   600	-2=p_1p_3-p_2,qquad p_2=2-3p_3<0.             \tag{R5}
   601	\]
   602	
   603	There is a printed subscript error: Lemma 2.6 says “essential for
   604	(\widetilde g_1),” whereas the analogue and (R5) require “essential for
   605	(\widetilde g_2).”  The repair is therefore
   606	`REPAIRED[LEMMA-2.6-g1-TO-g2]`; the printed statement with its literal
   607	(g_1) hypothesis is not what the proof establishes.
   608	
   609	**R-5, Lemmas 2.7–2.9 (pp. 5–6, (2.3)–(2.10)) —
   610	REPLAYED-SOUND.**  At each essential path vertex, its branch of determinant
   611	(d_i>1) consumes the one possible non-unit non-root branch.  The branch on
   612	the opposite side from (\widetilde v) therefore has determinant one.  This
   613	justifies the undisplayed orientations in Figs. 3–4 and the assertions
   614	(l_1=\cdots=l_n=1) used in (2.7).  Substitution of (2.3) in (2.4) gives
   615	(p_2=d_1(\Sigma-1)).  Lemma 2.8 similarly gives
   616	(\det\operatorname {br}_{\widetilde q_1}(\widetilde v)=-3B+1<-1) in the
   617	inessential case and (-d(\widetilde q_1)-1<-1) in the essential case.
   618	The algebra from (2.7) through (2.10) checks term by term.
   619	
   620	**R-6, omitted Lemma 2.10 calculation (p. 6, (2.11)) — REPAIRED.**  Put
   621	(D_j=\prod_{q\le j}d_q).  With the root between
   622	(\widetilde k_n) and (\widetilde g_1), the opposite-side branch
   623	determinants are one and the terminal canonical value is (-2).  The edge
   624	formula gives
   625	
   626	\[
   627	x_i=-\frac12\left(d_i\prod_{q>i}d_q^2-\mu_i\right),\qquad
   628	x_0=\frac12(\det\widetilde L_\infty-D_n^2).
   629	\]
   630	
   631	Inserting these in the canonical formula and using
   632	
   633	\[
   634	\sum_{j=1}^n(d_j-1)D_{j-1}d_j(D_n/D_j)^2=D_n^2-D_n
   635	\]
   636	
   637	gives
   638	
   639	\[
   640	0=-4-\det\widetilde L_\infty-D_n+
   641	  \sum_{j=1}^n\mu_j(d_j-1)D_{j-1}.
   642	\]
   643	
   644	The tail with (\mu_j=1) telescopes to (D_n-D_p), which is exactly
   645	(2.11) after changing sign.
   646	
   647	**R-7, omitted Lemma 2.11 calculation (p. 7, (2.12)) — REPAIRED.**  This is
   648	not a formal copy of 2.9 because the root changes side.  Put (m=n-1),
   649	(D=D_m), (d=d_n), and (C=d^2+d+1).  The root condition and
   650	(\det(\widetilde k_n\widetilde g_2)=1) give
   651	
   652	\[
   653	\det\operatorname {br}_{\widetilde k_n}(\widetilde v)=-(d+1),\qquad
   654	\det\operatorname {br}_{\widetilde g_2}(\widetilde v)=-C,
   655	\]
   656	
   657	and hence, for (i\le m),
   658	
   659	\[
   660	x_i=-{1\over C}\left(d_i\prod_{q=i+1}^{n}d_q^2-\mu_i\right),\qquad
   661	x_0={1\over C}(\det\widetilde L_\infty-(Dd)^2).
   662	\]
   663	
   664	The canonical formula at (\widetilde g_1) and the same telescoping identity
   665	through (m) reduce to
   666	
   667	\[
   668	0=1+\det\widetilde L_\infty+(D+d)(d+1)
   669	 -\sum_{j=1}^{m}\mu_j(d_j-1)D_{j-1}.
   670	\]
   671	
   672	The tail (\mu_j=d+1) is ((d+1)(D-D_p)), leaving precisely (2.12).
   673	This repaired formula is first consumed in §7, outside this lane.
   674	
   675	**R-8, Lemma 2.12 (p. 7, (2.13)) — REPLAYED-SOUND.**  If the root is in
   676	neither (Q_1) nor (Q_2), all displayed subgraphs in the proof are
   677	positive.  For (i=1), the first edge formula has negative right-hand side
   678	and forces (\Delta_1<0).  The second rearranges as
   679	
   680	\[
   681	\det Q_1\Delta_1=-\det(\widetilde a\widetilde g_1)
   682	 +\det Q_2\det Q_3(\det\delta(\widetilde a\widetilde g_1))^2\ge0
   683	\]
   684	
   685	by (2.13) and (\det Q_2\ge1), a contradiction.  The (i=2) branch is
   686	symmetric.
   687	
   688	### 4.2 Section 3 location claims
   689	
   690	The directional claims in Lemma 3.12(2)–(6), including the finite strict-left
   691	descent suppressed in part (6), are reconstructed in §2.3.  The p. 12 case-d
   692	cycle is reconstructed in §2.2.  They are **REPLAYED-SOUND** or **REPAIRED**
   693	as typed there.
   694	
   695	The earlier incidence assertions are also closed.  In Lemma 3.7(a) (pp. 9–10)
   696	the induction follows the unique source-tree path over the ordered target
   697	vertices \(c_1,\ldots,h\); its displayed determinant calculation gives the
   698	one incident nonconstant vertex.  Part (b)'s branch containing \(h^0\) is
   699	that unique path, while the other branch is linear and positive by
   700	Proposition 1.3 and [4], Lemma 5.  Assertion 3.9 then uses (3.1) and the
   701	local degree formula [4], (4): the \(m(\widetilde h)\) incident
   702	\(U^0\)-components each have determinant and local multiplicity one.
   703	These incidence conclusions are **REPLAYED-SOUND**.  They still do not imply
   704	Corollary 3.8(b)'s root exclusion, treated separately below.
   705	
   706	The two implicit Lemma 3.13 branch routings are replayed in §3.1; both are
   707	**REPAIRED** there by (3.1)–(3.2) saturation and the determinant-\(1/2\)
   708	contradiction.
   709	
   710	Two actual root-location gaps remain.
   711	
   712	**GAP-CANDIDATE[ROOT-U-LAST-CHAIN] (Corollary 3.8(b), p. 10).**  The exact
   713	quote is: “If \(\widetilde U\) is incident to \(\widetilde g_1\), then
   714	\(\widetilde v\notin\delta(\widetilde g_1\widetilde h)\).”  It is followed
   715	only by a citation to Lemmas 2.3, 2.4, and 3.7(b).  Lemmas 2.3–2.4 require
   716	both
   717	
   718	\[
   719	\det(\widetilde q\widetilde g_1)=
   720	\det\delta(\widetilde q\widetilde g_1)=1
   721	\]
   722	
   723	at a selected (\widetilde q).  Lemma 3.7(b) gives determinant one for a
   724	whole lifted (U_c)-component, not these two path determinants.  More
   725	decisively, it supplies positivity only for the exceptional branch not
   726	containing (h^0).  If the root is in the final linear segment toward
   727	(h^0), Lemma 2.4 merely declares that same segment negative; no displayed
   728	fact contradicts it.  No last vertex or endpoint equation handles this
   729	case.  **Verdict: GAP.**
   730	
   731	**GAP-CANDIDATE[ROOT-DELTA-G2-APPLICATION] (Lemma 3.15, pp. 14–15).**  The
   732	exact first conclusion is: “Then \(\widetilde v\notin
   733	\delta(\widetilde a\widetilde g_2)\).”  The proof establishes positivity of
   734	the whole delta graph, linearity of certain components, and
   735	(\det(\widetilde s\widetilde g_2)=2), then invokes Lemmas 2.5–2.6.  It
   736	never establishes their hypothesis
   737	(\det\delta(\widetilde s\widetilde g_2)=1) for each possible selected
   738	(\widetilde s), never checks the corrected (g_2)-essential-vertex
   739	hypothesis of Lemma 2.6, and never converts (R5)'s negative branch
   740	determinant into a contradiction with positivity of the whole delta graph.
   741	Linearity alone does not make an open-chain determinant one.  **Verdict:
   742	GAP.**  Its uses are forward dependencies in §§5–7.
   743	
   744	The two root-sign steps in Lemma 3.16 (p. 15) are
   745	**REPLAYED-SOUND**.  A lifted branch excluding the root is positive by [4],
   746	Lemma 4.  At a nodal (\widetilde b\subset
   747	\widetilde L_{\widetilde a}-U^0), the lifted left and lower branches both
   748	exclude the root, so [4], Lemma 3 gives
   749	
   750	\[
   751	\min(\det\widetilde L_{\widetilde b},
   752	     \det\widetilde D_{\widetilde b})=1.
   753	\]
   754	
   755	Formula (6) and [4], Lemma 5 transfer those determinants to (L_b,D_b);
